                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "fa11d0c3-6d16-34ef-bc6d-7797b457506d",
                        "entry_id": "091871c5-e000-3813-8ca4-28033cb9ba5f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Chambre particulière ou secrète dans un temple."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Ancient"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8651f5e8-e5bd-44ee-bf94-fb83d3668858",
                                "sense_id": "fa11d0c3-6d16-34ef-bc6d-7797b457506d",
                                "text": "Lorsque la porte d'entrée est à l’est, l’adytum est reculé sensiblement en arrière vers l’ouest, et il est déplacé d’une bien moins grande quantité à droite, vers le nord."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "71dd7fb4-1520-49a7-b31d-403214025bff",
                        "source_entry_id": "091871c5-e000-3813-8ca4-28033cb9ba5f",
                        "source_sense_id": "fa11d0c3-6d16-34ef-bc6d-7797b457506d",
                        "target_lang_word": "Adyton",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "1475a08f-7a05-46e8-ad04-6a30a46587a0",
                        "source_entry_id": "091871c5-e000-3813-8ca4-28033cb9ba5f",
                        "source_sense_id": "fa11d0c3-6d16-34ef-bc6d-7797b457506d",
                        "target_lang_word": "adytum",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "adytum",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2cbaf925-453f-3d0c-a234-1c4650b5eae6",
                                        "entry_id": "ad39c618-3c58-3397-a732-1594b67e9abf",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The innermost sanctuary or shrine in a temple, from where oracles were given."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a947c1da-0ab2-4cc7-afd3-cc3320bbc759",
                                                "sense_id": "2cbaf925-453f-3d0c-a234-1c4650b5eae6",
                                                "text": "Let us now with minds free of paſſion, enter the adytum with an intent to find out its true figure, to examine what it really was, and what it is. […] This point is properly the door-way or entrance into the adytum, as a wicket or little door, whilſt the jambs of the hithermoſt trilithons preſent themſelves, as the greater door, of about 40 feet wide, 25 cubits. […] [T]he more ſacred part of the temple at Hierapolis anſwering to our Adytum, had no door, tho' none enter'd therein but the chief prieſts."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "89f63e56-068e-4e93-be90-bf82ec45a06d",
                                                "sense_id": "2cbaf925-453f-3d0c-a234-1c4650b5eae6",
                                                "text": "At the diſtance of about forty ſtadia from this temple of Æſculapius, there is an incloſure; and in it there is an adytum ſacred to Iſis. This is the moſt holy of every thing which the Greeks conſecrate to this goddeſs. For the Tithoreans neither think it proper to take up their reſidence there, nor to ſuffer any to enter the adytum, except ſuch as the goddeſs Iſis informs them by a dream ſhe thinks proper to admit."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "59396112-39fb-394e-81a1-f199ccdebb4f",
                                        "entry_id": "ad39c618-3c58-3397-a732-1594b67e9abf",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A private chamber; a sanctum."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "broadly"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5d400a63-35b2-462d-abcb-96306a4ae103",
                                                "sense_id": "59396112-39fb-394e-81a1-f199ccdebb4f",
                                                "text": "Although it was the custom for the priestesses to affect a holy fury, a species of temporary insanity, in the delivering of their oracles, yet it was also the custom to write them on the leaves of trees, and to deposit them at the entrances of their caves, or the adyta of their temples; and it was the object of the devotee to secure them before they were dispersed by the winds; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d4201dd5-c819-454e-a7c9-1a110c4cb177",
                                                "sense_id": "59396112-39fb-394e-81a1-f199ccdebb4f",
                                                "text": "The adytums or inner circles of Abury and Stonehenge bear such an analogy to the holy of holies in Solomon's temple, as to induce the belief that they were formed subsequently to the temple of Jerusalem, with which the Tyrian workmen were quite familiar."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "44d67b5a-3c62-4ba4-ae03-fbe9f15c7ffc",
                                                "sense_id": "59396112-39fb-394e-81a1-f199ccdebb4f",
                                                "text": "[page 185] Before ascending the river to the Falls, I went up a beautiful clear stream that enters the Mississippi two miles above the capital, to visit Fountain Cave, a remarkable cavern out of which this tiny river flows. […] [page 186] The scene in the interior, illuminated by torches, and contemplated by an excited imagination, was truly enchanting; and I was anxious to penetrate the gloomy adytum still further."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2f93d1e0-027c-40b1-aa26-af6045c157b9",
                                                "sense_id": "59396112-39fb-394e-81a1-f199ccdebb4f",
                                                "text": "The ground plan, which I now lay before the Society, shows all that is left of the temple of Somnath, the astytar mundup, namely, and the adytum. The building adheres very nearly to the usual form of a Goozerat temple, as above described, but is larger than most examples, and contains some interesting \"episodes of plan,\" as they have been termed. The most happy of these occurs in the prudukshunâ or aisle for circumambulation around the adytum."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "75d4e35a-350e-4766-80db-fb59580e4728",
                                                "sense_id": "59396112-39fb-394e-81a1-f199ccdebb4f",
                                                "text": "They [the Hermetists] met for instruction in a sacred place, an adytum or shrine, which was apparently set apart solely for this purpose, and where they believed they could create conditions suitable for the reception of the inspiration of the Divine Mind by the instructor and for the handing of it on to the pupils— […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "20343dbb-6e07-4dac-812d-8b928a427d26",
                        "source_entry_id": "091871c5-e000-3813-8ca4-28033cb9ba5f",
                        "source_sense_id": "fa11d0c3-6d16-34ef-bc6d-7797b457506d",
                        "target_lang_word": "adyton",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "adyton",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4305d6c8-d5bd-32dd-8f93-da9c9d614ac8",
                                        "entry_id": "c8acfd32-d067-373f-b1e6-96d8820a103d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Synonym of adytum."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7d02d26a-79e9-4bcc-9391-cd10cda91d31",
                                                "sense_id": "4305d6c8-d5bd-32dd-8f93-da9c9d614ac8",
                                                "text": "The temple had no opisthodomos as usually understood, but this name was given at Didyma to the west wall of the adyton. The adyton floor was covered with a pavement in later centuries, but in the third century B.C. a grove of bay trees, it appears, grew in the adyton (and this is probably true of the archaic Didymeion)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7aebee48-ad21-4d94-8091-fd93b55beffe",
                                                "sense_id": "4305d6c8-d5bd-32dd-8f93-da9c9d614ac8",
                                                "text": "1998 [Routledge], Rodney Castleden, Atlantis Destroyed, 2001, Taylor & Francis (Routledge), Paperback, page 101,\nHere too is the only adyton so far discovered at Akrotiri. There are four in the temple at Knossos, and because the Akrotiri adyton is embellished with frescoes illuminating its use it is a crucial example, throwing light on the way the four adyta at Knossos were used."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "06cbf5ae-1357-4ce2-86e5-ad5222abaa53",
                                                "sense_id": "4305d6c8-d5bd-32dd-8f93-da9c9d614ac8",
                                                "text": "Others with similar elaborate adytons are at Qasr Neba and Sfira in Lebanon."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "5a8a8624-aa5f-4c17-b333-412b24e52c4e",
